<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adams–Onís Treaty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1819">1819</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Transcontinental Treaty, Florida Purchase Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Adams–Onís Treaty, signed in Washington on 22 February 1819 by the American secretary of state John Quincy Adams and the Spanish minister Luis de Onís, transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It also fixed the boundary between the United States and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> to the Pacific. The United States assumed up to five million dollars of its citizens' claims against Spain, and the treaty took effect in 1821.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adams-onis-treaty.jpg" alt="Adams–Onís Treaty" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Adams_onis_map.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Pinckney's Treaty of 1795 had set the borders between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and Spanish Florida, but expansion by American settlers led to continuing disputes, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louisiana-purchase">Louisiana Purchase</a> of 1803 added arguments over whether the boundary to the south ran along the Sabine River or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-wars-of-independence">Spanish American wars of independence</a> undermined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>'s hold on its American territories, and between 1810 and 1813 the United States annexed West Florida as far east as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perdido-river">Perdido River</a>.</p><p>During the first Seminole War of 1817–1818 it occupied the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, which gave John Quincy Adams, then Secretary of State, an opening to negotiate. By 1819 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> was ready to accept the loss of Florida in exchange for a firm border in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Negotiations were held in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, between Adams and the Spanish ambassador Luis de Onís, and produced an agreement of 16 articles. Article 2 transferred East and West Florida to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, and Article 3 defined the southern border between the two countries, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> withdrew its historic claims to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oregon-country">Oregon Country</a>. The United States renounced claims to Spanish Texas. Under Article 11 it set up a commission to handle claims by American citizens against Spain, which distributed around five million dollars before its dissolution in 1824.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The US Senate approved the treaty unanimously on 24 February 1819, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> delayed ratification until October 1820, hoping to dissuade the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from supporting anti-Spanish revolutionaries. The Senate voted again, and Henry Clay and others demanded the cession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> as well, a proposal that was defeated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> recognised Mexican independence in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-cordoba">Treaty of Córdoba</a> on 24 August 1821, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> accepted the boundary in the Treaty of Limits of 1828.</p><p>For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the treaty extended its claimed territory from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a> to the Pacific. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> it preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and a buffer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-mexico">New Mexico</a> and American territory. Many historians count it a great achievement for the United States, since Adams's vision of trade with the Orient across the Pacific was later borne out. The treaty was formally terminated in 1902.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Adams–Onís Treaty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Unable to defend the territory, Spain negotiated: the <b>Adams–Onís Treaty</b> of 22 February 1819 took effect on 17 July 1821, and hundreds of Black Seminoles sailed from Cape Florida to the Bahamas to avoid being returned to bondage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>As secretary of state he orchestrated the Treaty of 1818, negotiated the <b>Transcontinental Treaty</b> of 1819 and formulated the Monroe Doctrine of 1823.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/manifest-destiny">Manifest destiny · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Florida, once the major part of the Spanish territory of La Florida and ceded to the United States in the 1819 <b>Adams–Onís Treaty</b>, became an organised incorporated territory.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
